docs/ENV.md	| `WARDYN_EXEC_OUTPUT_TAIL` | bool | `true` | keep the last `WARDYN_RUN_OUTPUT_TAIL_BYTES` of every non-interactive run's combined stdout/stderr, in wardynd's memory while the run lives and, with `WARDYN_RUN_OUTPUT_PERSIST` on, in Postgres once it ends (the managed-harness sign-in run keeps none), readable through `GET /api/v1/runs/{id}/output` by the run's owner or an admin (flag `-exec-output-tail`). Independent of `WARDYN_RECORDING_STORE`: it works with recordings `off`. `off` (or any false value) keeps none and refuses rows stored earlier, and the route then answers `409` (`run_output_off`). Masked like a recording, but a secret the command prints that Wardyn never registered is kept like any log line — see OPERATIONS.md "Run output" |	a Kubernetes run's output is not captured, and the row says so (654710062)
docs/ENV.md	| `WARDYN_PG_DSN` 🔒 | string | (unset, required) | Postgres DSN (flag `-dsn`). pgx's own `pool_max_conns` DSN param, not a `WARDYN_*` var, so it is not its own row here — but operators should size it **at least 3, and at least 5 with the ground-truth rotator enabled**: the single-instance lock (`db.SingleInstanceLockKey`) holds one connection for the whole process lifetime (wardynd refuses to start while another instance holds it; `WARDYN_HA` lifts that, and then no replica holds it), the sweeper leader's lock (`db.SweeperLeaderLockKey`, which elects the one replica that runs the approval, recording, credential, always-egress and pause sweepers) holds one for the whole process lifetime too — on a pool below 3 it cannot spare the connection, so that process sweeps without an election and a warning says so, which is only safe for one replica, and `WARDYN_HA` refuses to start on a pool below 3 — the lifecycle reaper borrows one per tick (transient, released immediately after), and the ground-truth rotator's leader-election lock (`db.GroundTruthRotatorLockKey`) holds one for the whole process lifetime once acquired — all on top of whatever the pool needs for its own request-serving queries. At 2, one person's concurrent sign-ins are not serialized — each such pass writes an `auth.signin_unserialized` audit row — so give it at least 3 to serialize them. An organisation that enrols laptops (hybrid enrolment) should add headroom for them: each enrolled device's audit push holds one connection while its claimed hashes are recomputed, a device has at most one push in flight (a second concurrent push is answered 429 with `Retry-After`), so budget one connection per device you expect to push at the same moment. **Lock connections, on top of `pool_max_conns`:** the cross-replica locks (the per-run operation lock, the site-configuration and capability-enforcement writers, the audit chain verify sweep, and the AWS SSO and Azure DevOps sign-in refresh single-flights; `internal/db/locks.go`) each hold a connection of their own, dialled from the same DSN and kept out of the request pool, so a slow guarded operation can never starve the queries it guards. A process holds at most **8** at once (`db.LockPoolMaxConns`, fixed, not configurable); nested locks share their caller's connection, and a lock that finds all 8 in use waits two seconds and is then refused with `503` `lock_unavailable` (retry), never skipped. Budget Postgres `max_connections` for `pool_max_conns` plus 8 lock connections plus 2 listener connections per replica (every wardynd dials `LISTEN wardyn_live` and `LISTEN wardyn_mask` outside the pool, with `WARDYN_HA` or not); the sign-in supersede lock is the one exception, and keeps its documented behaviour on a pooled connection (see above) |	a DSN without pool_max_conns now gets a pool of 10 or the CPU count, and the row says so (839270d03)
